VPN Constraints and Meeting Kickoff: The meeting opened with participants discussing bandwidth issues and virtual private network (VPN) limitations, where Tin noted that the VPN allows meeting access but restricts email and chat capabilities (00:00:01). Carl transitioned the discussion to review the business review standard operating procedure (SOP) they created, proposing a teach-back format where participants teach back their learnings from the reviewed document (00:01:05).
WBR Deck Structure and Business Signals: Following Carl's prompt for a teach-back, Tin summarized the all-encompassing Weekly Business Review (WBR) deck, explaining that it covers all lines of business, identifies areas needing focus over weekly or monthly periods, utilizes white paper business signals, and highlights high-level performance views alongside per-site components and variations (00:02:21).
Automation Script Development for WBR: Building on the WBR document summary, Jian proposed building an automation script to generate the report by Thursday or Friday of the week while incorporating the document's logic, noting that manual generation by business intelligence developers on Mondays is tedious (00:05:44).
Week-on-Week Changes and Magnitude Thresholds: Carl and Tin discussed week-on-week metric changes, where Carl explained that material changes are defined by specific basis point movements (where 1 point equals 100 basis points) and require isolating whether a decline is a new normal or an isolated event (00:07:23). Carl detailed specific magnitude thresholds for percentage KPIs: movements under 50 basis points are normal variations requiring no callout, 50 to 99 basis points serve as Amber Alert signal candidates, 1 to 1.99 percentage points require urgent investigation led by Wes, and movements over 200 basis points involve directors and client services (00:11:51).
Persistence and Action Urgency in Metrics: Carl explained how to handle metric persistence, noting that consecutive negative movements totaling at least 50 basis points over multiple weeks should be treated as signals requiring immediate team alignment, with Jian sounding the alarm and Wes driving the investigation and intervention (00:15:59).
AHT Guidance, Metrics Correlation, and Deviations: Addressing Tin's concern regarding hard volume and Average Handle Time (AHT) metrics, Carl advised using statistically sound historical data to establish normal deviations for AHT without applying basis points directly (00:17:25). While acknowledging the correlation between AHT and customer satisfaction ("smiley"), Carl noted that causation must still be proven, leading to a consensus that AHT will receive rehabilitative, isolated studies rather than rigorous performance improvement plan sprints until quality behavior markers are fully established in the first quarter (00:20:15).
Concurrency, Volume, and Environmental Assessment: Carl explained that volume is not heavily relied upon because workforce management shifts personnel, directing participants instead to use actual concurrency, application usage, and AHT standard deviations to spot abnormal spikes indicating environmental overwhelm (00:23:01). Carl and Tin outlined the operational assessment process for environment changes, determining whether busyness is caused by absenteeism or client-induced ticket elevations, concluding that operations must fix the environment rather than creating new customer satisfaction action plans when concurrency spikes (00:24:38).
Operational Scenario Analysis on Performance Dips: During a scenario quiz posed by Carl regarding normal volume and absenteeism paired with increased AHT and decreased quality and customer satisfaction, Tin suggested potential causes such as agent handle times or leadership presence, while Jian and Wes highlighted interaction complexity, policy changes, and technology latency, with Carl adding tenure mix changes and onboarding curves as a primary factor (00:27:05).
Forecasting New Hire Glide Paths and Preventing Client Surprises: Discussing how to prevent client surprises during predictable performance dips caused by new hire onboarding, Tin described monitoring live performance reactively, while Carl argued for a system-based approach through capacity planning and forecasting (00:32:21). Jian and Wes suggested balancing performance with tenured staff and forecasting glide paths, resulting in Carl's guidance that anticipated performance drops from new hires should be documented in trajectory forecasts as expected downturns rather than major events, provided they match glide path expectations (00:36:27).
Action Registry and Efficacy Validation: Reviewing the action registry managed by Wes, Carl established that operations must evaluate every action weekly and categorize them by efficacy: validated with clear evidence, partially effective, no observable effect, or insufficient evidence (00:42:20). Carl noted that low overall success rates will place operations on the spot during senior leadership team calls to revisit and audit their actions (00:44:54). Wes raised execution questions, confirming with Carl that improperly executed actions fall under no observable effect (00:46:09).
Global Scalability Criteria for Actions: Carl outlined the strict criteria required for an action to be scaled globally, stating it must demonstrate high impact with high repeatability, transferability across different lines of businesses and tenures, sustainability beyond initial intervention periods, and low operational, compliance, and security risks (00:47:10).
Filtering and Managing Action Plans Locally: Wes raised concerns about filtering action plans before they enter the log, but Carl and Tin agreed that internal and client-facing trackers should remain independent to preserve creativity and use unmeasured or ineffective actions as teachable moments for operations directors rather than sanitizing them away (00:48:20). Carl instructed Wes to return unmeasurable actions for revision (00:54:17).
Salesforce P0 Escalation Path and Governance: Carl introduced section 10 of the SOP regarding escalation paths, explaining that Salesforce P0 escalations will be utilized to notify divisional vice presidents directly and trigger ad hoc governance meetings and weekly calls for struggling sites, with Wes granted access to the tool (00:56:36).
Managing New Hire Performance and Client Expectations: The underlying problem involved managing client pressure caused by new hire performance impacts. Wes argued for starting with a training needs analysis, topic mastering, refreshers, and leveraging quick wins from tenure operators (00:59:07) (01:01:29). Carl countered that this approach was overly complex, explaining that the issue should instead be evaluated by checking if new hires were expected in the capacity file, whether bids captured performance forecasting, and if new hires performed within expected normal delivery curves week by week (01:00:14). Carl concluded that if proper projections and client expectations were established (such as a 6-week window to proficiency), operating sites should not face undue pressure, and persistent drops outside predicted paths indicate problems elsewhere (01:02:25).
Noise versus Signal and Business Forecasting: The discussion focused on separating operational noise from true performance signals to guide business operations. Carl stated that leadership must evaluate data using a 13-week historical and forward-looking window, factoring in predictable events such as Black Friday Cyber Monday volume increases and Q4 bottom performer reductions (01:03:27). Jian added that the defining file separates noise from signal, providing operations with structured direction rather than raw numbers (01:06:25).
